A process is an instance of a program in execution.  It includes the program code (text section), program counter, processor registers, stack, and data section. Processes can be in different states: new, ready, running, waiting, and terminated. Process management involves creating, scheduling, and terminating processes. The Process Control Block (PCB) is a data structure that contains information about a process, such as its ID, state, priority, and memory pointers. Understanding process concepts is crucial for understanding how operating systems manage concurrent execution of programs. 

```c
// Example: Creating a new process using fork()
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
  pid_t pid = fork();
  if (pid == 0) {
    printf("Child process\n");
  } else if (pid > 0) {
    printf("Parent process\n");
  } else {
    fprintf(stderr, "Fork failed\n");
  }
  return 0;
}
```
